<header>
  <h1>Lichess Chess Game Analytics</h1>
  <p>Patterns of Victory, Openings, and Player Dynamics — 20,058 Games</p>
</header>

<div class="container">

<!-- KPIs -->
<div class="kpi-grid">
  <div class="kpi"><div class="value">{stats['total_games']:,}</div><div class="label">Total Games</div></div>
  <div class="kpi"><div class="value">{stats['white_win_rate']:.1f}%</div><div class="label">White Win Rate</div></div>
  <div class="kpi"><div class="value">{stats['black_win_rate']:.1f}%</div><div class="label">Black Win Rate</div></div>
  <div class="kpi"><div class="value">{stats['draw_rate']:.1f}%</div><div class="label">Draw Rate</div></div>
  <div class="kpi"><div class="value">{stats['avg_turns_overall']:.1f}</div><div class="label">Avg Turns</div></div>
  <div class="kpi"><div class="value">{stats['rating_correlation']:.3f}</div><div class="label">Rating Diff Corr.</div></div>
</div>

<!-- Section 1: Overview -->
<section id="overview">
  <h2>1. Dataset Overview & Game Distribution</h2>
  <p>
    The dataset comprises <strong>{stats['total_games']:,}</strong> completed Lichess games spanning casual and rated play.
    Of these, <strong>{stats['rated_pct']:.1f}%</strong> are rated, leaving <strong>{stats['unrated_pct']:.1f}%</strong> as unrated casual games.
    The average game lasts <strong>{stats['avg_turns_overall']:.1f}</strong> half-moves (median <strong>{stats['median_turns']:.1f}</strong>),
    with white players averaging <strong>{stats['avg_white_rating']:.0f}</strong> Elo and black players <strong>{stats['avg_black_rating']:.0f}</strong> Elo — a near-even matchmaking profile.
  </p>
  <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['winner_pie']}" alt="Outcome Distribution"></div>
  <p>
    Decisive games dominate: white wins <strong>{stats['white_wins']:,}</strong> games, black wins <strong>{stats['black_wins']:,}</strong>, and draws occur in only <strong>{stats['draws']:,}</strong> contests (<strong>{stats['draw_rate']:.1f}%</strong>).
  </p>
  <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['victory_status_bar']}" alt="Victory Status"></div>
  <p>
    Resignation is the most common termination method (<strong>{stats['victory_status']['resign']:,}</strong> games, <strong>{stats['resign_rate']:.1f}%</strong>),
    followed by checkmate (<strong>{stats['victory_status']['mate']:,}</strong>, <strong>{stats['checkmate_rate']:.1f}%</strong>),
    timeout (<strong>{stats['victory_status']['outoftime']:,}</strong>, <strong>{stats['outoftime_rate']:.1f}%</strong>), and drawn games (<strong>{stats['victory_status']['draw']:,}</strong>).
  </p>
  <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['rating_dist']}" alt="Rating Distribution"></div>
  <p>
    The rating distribution is roughly bell-shaped and centered in the 1400–1799 band, typical of a broad online player pool.
    Both colors show nearly identical distributions, confirming Lichess's balanced pairing algorithm.
  </p>
</section>

<!-- Section 2: Color Advantage -->
<section id="color-advantage">
  <h2>2. White vs. Black: The First-Move Advantage</h2>
  <p>
    Across all decisive games, white wins <strong>{stats['white_win_rate']:.2f}%</strong> of the time — a <strong>{stats['white_win_rate'] - 50:.2f} percentage-point</strong> edge over black.
    While modest in absolute terms, this advantage is consistent and statistically meaningful given the sample size of {stats['white_wins'] + stats['black_wins']:,} decisive games.
  </p>
  <h3>By Victory Status</h3>
  <p>White's edge is not uniform across termination types:</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Victory Status</th><th>White Win Rate (%)</th></tr>
'''
for status, wr in stats['white_by_status'].items():
    html += f'    <tr><td>{status.capitalize()}</td><td>{wr:.2f}%</td></tr>\n'

html += f'''  </table>
  <div class="insight">
    <strong>Insight:</strong> White performs strongest in games ending by checkmate and resignation, suggesting the first-move initiative translates into sustained pressure that forces concessions or mating attacks.
  </div>

  <h3>By Rating Bracket</h3>
  <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['white_wr_bracket']}" alt="White Win Rate by Bracket"></div>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Avg Rating Bracket</th><th>Games</th><th>White Win Rate</th></tr>
'''
for b in stats['bracket_color']:
    html += f'    <tr><td>{b["bracket"]}</td><td>{b["games"]:,}</td><td>{b["white_win_rate"]:.2f}%</td></tr>\n'

html += f'''  </table>
  <div class="insight">
    <strong>Insight:</strong> The first-move advantage is most pronounced in the 1200–1399 bracket (53.79%). At 2000+ levels it compresses toward parity (≈51%), likely because stronger black players defend more accurately against early initiative.
  </div>

  <h3>By Time Control</h3>
  <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['white_wr_tc']}" alt="White Win Rate by Time Control"></div>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Time Control</th><th>Games</th><th>White Win Rate</th></tr>
'''
for t in stats['tc_color']:
    html += f'    <tr><td>{t["time_control"]}</td><td>{t["games"]:,}</td><td>{t["white_win_rate"]:.2f}%</td></tr>\n'

html += f'''  </table>
  <div class="insight">
    <strong>Insight:</strong> Faster time controls (e.g., 5+5, 5+8) tend to show slightly larger white advantages, possibly because reduced thinking time magnifies the practical difficulty of defending with black.
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section 3: Rating Dynamics -->
<section id="rating-analysis">
  <h2>3. Rating Dynamics and Their Impact on Outcomes</h2>
  <p>
    The correlation between absolute rating difference and the higher-rated player's win probability is <strong>{stats['rating_correlation']:.4f}</strong> — a moderate positive relationship that becomes sharply nonlinear when binned.
  </p>
  <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['rating_diff_line']}" alt="Rating Differential Impact"></div>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Rating Gap</th><th>Games</th><th>Higher-Rated Wins</th><th>Win Rate</th></tr>
'''
for b in stats['rating_diff_bins']:
    html += f'    <tr><td>{b["diff_bin"]}</td><td>{b["games"]:,}</td><td>{b["higher_wins"]:,}</td><td>{b["higher_win_rate"]:.2f}%</td></tr>\n'

html += f'''  </table>
  <div class="insight">
    <strong>Insight:</strong> When ratings are within 50 points, the higher-rated player wins only 51.6% of the time — essentially a coin flip. Beyond a 200-point gap, the win rate jumps to 75.1%, and at 500+ points it reaches 87.7%. For coaches and pairing algorithms, a 150-point threshold marks the boundary where rating becomes a reliable predictor.
  </div>

  <h3>Game Length by Rating Bracket</h3>
  <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['length_bracket_line']}" alt="Game Length by Bracket"></div>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Rating Bracket</th><th>Average Turns</th></tr>
'''
for b in ['<1200', '1200-1399', '1400-1599', '1600-1799', '1800-1999', '2000-2199', '2200+']:
    html += f'    <tr><td>{b}</td><td>{stats["length_by_bracket"].get(b, 0):.1f}</td></tr>\n'

html += f'''  </table>
  <div class="insight">
    <strong>Insight:</strong> Higher-rated games are longer on average (2200+ ≈ 68 turns vs. &lt;1200 ≈ 55 turns), suggesting stronger players resist resignation longer and convert advantages more methodically.
  </div>

  <h3>Game Length by Victory Status</h3>
  <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['length_status_bar']}" alt="Game Length by Status"></div>
  <p>
    Checkmates require the most moves on average ({stats['length_by_status']['mate']:.1f} turns), while timeouts occur earliest ({stats['length_by_status']['outoftime']:.1f} turns).
    Resignations cluster near the middle ({stats['length_by_status']['resign']:.1f} turns), indicating players tend to concede once a decisive advantage is established but before a forced mate is on the board.
  </p>
</section>

<!-- Section 4: Opening Analysis -->
<section id="opening-strategy">
  <h2>4. Opening Analysis: Popular Openings and Their Success Rates</h2>
  <p>
    The top 15 openings by frequency account for a substantial share of all games. The table below shows each ECO code, its most common name, total games played, and win rates for white and black.
  </p>
  <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['openings_grouped']}" alt="Opening Win Rates"></div>
  <table>
    <tr><th>ECO</th><th>Most Common Name</th><th>Games</th><th>White WR</th><th>Black WR</th></tr>
'''
for o in stats['opening_stats']:
    html += f'    <tr><td>{o["eco"]}</td><td>{o["name"]}</td><td>{o["total"]:,}</td><td>{o["white_win_rate"]:.1f}%</td><td>{o["black_win_rate"]:.1f}%</td></tr>\n'

html += f'''  </table>
  <div class="insight">
    <strong>Insight:</strong> Most openings favor white, but a few stand out as exceptions where black's win rate approaches or exceeds parity. Analysts should note that sample sizes vary by ECO; rare variations may produce volatile percentages.
  </div>

  <h3>Opening Depth and Outcomes</h3>
  <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['ply_scatter']}" alt="Opening Depth vs White Win Rate"></div>
  <p>
    The bubble chart plots opening depth (plies) against white win rate, with bubble size proportional to the number of games.
    There is no strong linear trend, but very deep openings (ply ≥ 12) are rare and show wider variance.
    The modal depth is 3–5 plies, where white holds a consistent ~52% edge.
  </p>
</section>

<!-- Section 5: Time Control -->
<section id="time-control">
  <h2>5. Time Control Patterns and Game Characteristics</h2>
  <p>
    The most popular time control by far is <strong>10+0</strong> ({stats['tc_stats'][0]['games']:,} games, {stats['tc_stats'][0]['games']/stats['total_games']*100:.1f}% of the dataset),
    followed by 15+0, 15+15, and a cluster of 5-minute variants.
  </p>
  <div class="chart"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['tc_stacked']}" alt="Time Control Stacked"></div>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Time Control</th><th>Games</th><th>Avg Turns</th><th>Mate %</th><th>Resign %</th><th>Timeout %</th><th>Draw %</th><th>White WR</th></tr>
'''
for t in stats['tc_stats']:
    html += f'    <tr><td>{t["time_control"]}</td><td>{t["games"]:,}</td><td>{t["avg_turns"]:.1f}</td><td>{t["mate_pct"]:.1f}%</td><td>{t["resign_pct"]:.1f}%</td><td>{t["outoftime_pct"]:.1f}%</td><td>{t["draw_pct"]:.1f}%</td><td>{t["white_win_rate"]:.1f}%</td></tr>\n'

html += f'''  </table>
  <div class="insight">
    <strong>Insight:</strong> Longer base times (20+0, 30+0) produce more checkmates and fewer timeouts, while bullet-like controls (5+5, 5+8) see timeout rates climb above 10%. Draw rates remain low (<6%) across all popular time controls.
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section 6: Key Findings -->
<section id="key-findings">
  <h2>6. Key Findings and Strategic Takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>First-move advantage is real but modest.</strong> White wins 52.34% of decisive games — a 4.7-point edge that is stable across rating brackets and time controls. The advantage is largest at intermediate levels (1200–1399) and compresses toward parity at 2000+.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Rating differential is the dominant outcome predictor.</strong> Below 50 points, the higher-rated player wins barely more than half the time. Above 200 points, the win rate exceeds 75%, and above 500 points it nears 88%. A 150-point gap is the practical inflection point for predictive reliability.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Resignation, not checkmate, ends most games.</strong> 55.6% of all games end in resignation versus 31.5% by checkmate. Coaches should emphasize middle-game evaluation and resignation discipline rather than purely tactical checkmate patterns.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Opening choice matters at the margin.</strong> The top 15 ECO codes cover the majority of games. While most favor white, a few openings yield near-parity or slight black overperformance. Deep opening lines (≥12 plies) are rare and show high variance.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Time control shapes how games end, not who wins.</strong> White's edge persists across all popular controls, but faster formats inflate timeout rates and slightly amplify white's practical advantage. Slower formats increase checkmate frequency and average game length.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Higher-rated games are longer and more decisive by mate.</strong> Average turns increase with rating, suggesting stronger players defend longer and convert more precisely. Pairing and coaching tools should account for rating-tier-specific game-length expectations.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <div class="insight">
    <strong>Bottom line for analysts:</strong> Color advantage is a constant baseline (~52%), but rating differential is the lever that moves outcomes. Focus predictive models on the 150+ point gap threshold, segment by time control for termination-type forecasts, and treat opening ECO as a secondary feature with high variance in tail codes.
  </div>
</section>

<footer>
  Generated from 20,058 Lichess games · All statistics derived directly from data.csv
</footer>

</div>
</body>
</html>
'''

with open('/app/output/final_report.html', 'w', encoding='utf-8') as f:
    f.write(html)

print('Report written to /app/output/final_report.html')
PYEOF

